A||N||3||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Work completed!  2.5L Iron Duke Head Gasket||||14||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-20-2001||03:39 AM||||The new head gasket (NEW HEAD BOLTS) is installed in my 1984 Fiero 2m4 sc.  It took a lot of time and hard work, but the engine runs quieter and with much more power than before.  There were lots of other fixes and improvements completed along the way.<P>1.  Deburr intake and exhaust passages<BR>2.  Replace thremostat housing<BR>3.  New plugs and wires<BR>4.  New EGR and TBI gaskets<BR>5.  New TPS sensor<BR>6.  Added exhaust manifold gasket between head and exhaust manifold heat shield.  This was in addition to the heat shield to exhaust manifold gasket.<BR>7.  New air and fuel filters<BR>8.  New PCV and crankcase vent<BR>9.  Rebuilt alternator and starter<BR>10. New belts and some hoses where needed<P>The intake and exhaust manifolds were loose and showed signs of leaking when I removed them.  The head was replaced 2yrs/5,000 miles ago by pros, but they did a lousy job.  <P>I bought the car about a month ago.  The compression I measured in each of the four cylinders was 170, 120, 118, and 165 psig.  The car hesitated and leaked coolant from the thremostat housing which was badly warped at the flange.  <P>Now it pulls past 5,000 rpm and it has a lot more acceleration, especially from 40 to 65mph where it was weak before.  Now that the exhaust is sealed, the car is much quieter.<BR>  <BR>I did not port-match.  Rather, the idea was to just make the intake and exhaust passages smooth. Their diameter and shape were not modified.  <P>How much power could a minimal deburring of the intake and exhaust passages add?  My guess is that it might add 8 to 10 hp.  That might mean my little duke is good for 100hp.  I expect a monolithic catalytic converter will add at least 5 more. <P>I am considering an Accel 300+ digital ignition system.  Could the combination of these "slight modifications" lead to a 110HP duke?     ||||reg||14
Z||000001||MrPBody||01-20-2001||10:33 AM||||You got yours done faster than I have!<P>Today I am going to get a rebuilt head; mine was cracked.  (I mean the Duke's head; my head may still be cracked.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||01-20-2001||12:24 PM||||You probably gained 1-3 horsepower because you deburred your intake and exhaust. The two middle cylinders pressures were low, which are right next to each other. This indicates to me that your head gasket between those two cylinders was leaking causing the compression loss in those two cylinders. Most of the power gain you feel is because you may have fixed this. I'll bet if you do another compression check that those cylinders will have pressure more like the outer two did, normal.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Motorhead||01-20-2001||03:01 PM||||I've found that our Duke responds well to just about anything you do to the ignition system.  My new distributor was worth at least 10HP.  Any airflow improvements benefit greatly. My head and intake were ported!  But keep in mind that this engine is an "econo-motor"!  It does what it was designed to do with great efficiancy!  IF you take care of it!<BR> How many miles are you showing?<BR>I have removed the catalytic converter and run a stock muffler.  The car runs STRONG and idles flawlessly!<BR>My 2 cents.<BR>Mike<P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe||||reg||1
